    "description": "Shipping's persistence layer must handle Customer, Shipment, and Delivery records across multiple requests and worker processes. A customer may be loaded by the account service, cached by the rate service, and loaded again by the shipment service during the same session. Updates and cancellations must always affect the stored record, and a Shipment must retain a usable reference to its Customer even when the Customer object is not loaded. The current mapper creates a new in-memory object on every query and cannot reliably determine whether two copies refer to the same record. Define how each persistent entity carries a stable database handle so repeated loads, cache lookups, updates, deletions, and references all resolve to the same stored entity. Transient request-response payloads and immutable calculation results are out of scope and should not receive persistent identity semantics.",
    "decision_statement": "Decide where and how persistent entities retain their stable database identifier so repeated loads, references, cache entries, updates, and deletions resolve to the same stored record.",
    "notes": "Dropped association-table-mapping: the scenario has no many-to-many association, so a link table is not defensible; kept foreign-key-mapping as the near miss for the Shipment-to-Customer reference axis."
  },
